From: RetroOPI_Z3
Subject: [PATCH] aica: optionally run the AICA on its own thread

The AICA (its ARM7 and the sample generation) ran in lockstep with the
SH4, one sample per 4535 SH4 cycles. When the SH4 runs below full speed
(Windows CE games such as Sega Rally 2 at ~55%), so did the sound, which
broke up. New core option "Threaded Audio" (default on): the AICA runs on
its own thread, paced by the wall clock at 44.1 kHz, pausing when the SH4
stops. The SH4 reaches the AICA
through readAicaReg/writeAicaReg and (de)serialize, which now take a
recursive mutex the AICA thread holds per 8-sample chunk. SH4 interrupts
raised on the AICA thread are delivered by the SH4 thread at its next
AICA tick. Disc reads (CDDA comes from the AICA) are serialised. The
thread is stopped whenever the emulator stops, resets or terminates, and
restarted from the SH4 side. In lockstep, the frontend's audio sync held
the SH4 to real time; with the AICA threaded the SH4 throttles itself, so
that emulated time never runs more than 50 ms ahead of the wall clock.

